<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Clements Kadalie</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1896–<a href="/years/1951">1951</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Clements Kadalie was a trade unionist from Nyasaland, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malawi">Malawi</a>, who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-and-commercial-workers-union">Industrial and Commercial Workers' Union</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a> in 1919. Under his leadership the ICU became the first mass movement of black workers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> in the 1920s, before it split and declined at the end of the decade. He later led an independent branch of the union in East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>He was born Lameck Koniwaka Kadali Muwamba in the village of Chifira near the Bandawe Mission Station in Nkhata Bay District, the second son of Musa Kadalie Muwamba and grandson of Chiweyu, a paramount chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonga">Tonga</a> of Nyasaland. Educated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-scotland">Church of Scotland</a> missionaries, he completed teacher training at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livingstonia">Livingstonia</a> in 1913 and taught briefly in a primary school before joining the stream of Nyasalanders seeking work in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> in early 1915.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a> in 1918 and befriended Arthur F. Batty, an emerging trade unionist and political activist, on whose advice he founded the Industrial and Commercial Union, later renamed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-and-commercial-workers-union">Industrial and Commercial Workers' Union</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, to protest against unfair labour laws and protect workers' rights. In December 1919 a dockworkers' strike of 2,000 men lasting fourteen days stopped all exports through Cape Town harbour and made him known across the country. By 1927 the union could claim a membership of one hundred thousand.</p><p>On 24 November 1924 he was arrested and served with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a> order naming him a prohibited immigrant and ordering him to leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> within three days. In May 1927 he represented the union at the international labour conference in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>. Internal fighting followed, and William G. Ballinger removed him with the full backing of the executive committee. In May 1928 he and six other union leaders were arrested under the Native Administration Act, which made it a criminal offence to arouse racial animosity towards the white population.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After forming his independent union in East London he was also a provincial organiser of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">African National Congress</a>. In 1921 he had married Johanna Davidson, a widow with two children, with whom he had four children; he later lived in East London with his second wife, Eva, and their son, Victor. He never returned to his homeland and died in 1951. His granddaughter Rhoda Kadalie (1953–2022) became an academic, columnist and director of the Impumelelo Social Innovations Centre.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Clements Kadalie” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…South African Native National Congress, renamed the African National Congress in 1923, was founded at Bloemfontein in 1912; <b>Clements Kadalie</b>’s Industrial and Commercial Workers’ Union organised tens of thousands of workers in the 1920s; and in the 1950s the ANC and its allies ran the Defiance Campaign of…</blockquote><a href="/empire/union-of-south-africa">Union of South Africa · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1931" aria-label="London on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4547 381 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Clements Kadalie"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4997 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4997 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1931">What was here in 1931? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
